// The keyboard, wired to the document.
//
// gfx_poll hands back one code per call: the byte a key produced, 256..265 for
// the arrows and the editing keys, that code plus 65536 for a release, and -1
// for nothing. A release is not an edit and neither is -1, so both fall off
// the end of the chain having done nothing, which is why no branch tests for
// them.
//
// The dispatch is a chain of two-way branches rather than one table because a
// block performs three actions and an if/else pair costs two of them.

ed_poll() {
  int v = gfx_poll();
  ed_key(v);
} return int v;
given ed_step_fixture ():(-1)
given ed_step_fixture ():(-1) then calls ed_key:(-1)

ed_key(int v) {
  if(v >= 32 && v < 127) {
    ed_type(v);
  } else {
    ed_key2(v);
  }
} return void;
given ed_step_fixture (8):() then calls ed_key2:(8)
given ed_step_fixture (200):() then calls ed_key2:(200)

ed_key2(int v) {
  if(v == 8 || v == 127) {
    ed_back();
  } else {
    ed_key3(v);
  }
} return void;
given ed_step_fixture (8):() then calls ed_back:()
given ed_step_fixture (200):() then calls ed_key3:(200)
